<h2>Designing Agents and Skills</h2>
<p>The harness forms the team automatically, but <strong>if you know the principles you can direct it more precisely</strong>, and you can also <strong>tune the generated team yourself</strong>. There are two key things — <strong>agents</strong> (who) and <strong>skills</strong> (how).</p>

<h3>An agent = a "job description" written in natural language</h3>
<p>An agent is not code but <strong>writing</strong>. It holds the following four things.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Role · principles</strong> — what it is responsible for, the rules it must keep.</li>
    <li><strong>Input · output</strong> — what it takes in and what it puts out (the protocol).</li>
    <li><strong>Collaboration</strong> — which agents it exchanges what with.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-invocation · behavior on errors</strong> — what to do when it gets stuck.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code><span class="c"># e.g. sales standard-source analyst (excerpt from an agent definition)</span>
Role: Analyze the credit and order flow of ERP sales/order, and
      identify the scope of impact this customization has on standard features.
Principles: Always put minimizing damage to the standard · upgrade compatibility first.
Input: client request (CR), standard specification/source references
Output: impact report (list of affected screens, features, data)
Collaboration: pass the impact to the designer, request re-investigation when information is lacking
Errors: if the evidence cannot be found, mark it "needs checking" and do not let it pass</code></pre>

<h3>A skill = a "manual" that holds a repetitive task</h3>
<p>A skill consists of <code>name + description + procedure · checklist</code>. A well-made skill gets <strong>reused</strong> by several agents.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># e.g. the "regression test checklist" skill</span>
name: regression-checklist
description: Check that the standard sales/order features are not broken after the customization is applied
procedure:
  1. Check that standard order creation/modification/cancellation works normally
  2. Check that orders within the normal credit limit range are not blocked
  3. Check the customization (hold on exceeding the limit) behavior
  4. Check the notification dispatch and the logs
  5. Cross-check numerical consistency (order amount · limit)</code></pre>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The 5 steps of skill design (beginner tip)</span>
    ① Find a task that repeats often → ② split it so it carries <strong>only one responsibility</strong> → ③ decide the input/output → ④ write down the exception handling → ⑤ connect it to the agent that will use it.
</div>

