<h3>Beginner principles for forming a good team</h3>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Why</th><th>ERP example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Keep roles <strong>narrow</strong></td><td>If one agent does many jobs, quality drops ↓</td><td>Separate "analysis" from "development"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Make input/output <strong>clear</strong></td><td>So the next person picks it up right away</td><td>Impact report → design input</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Keep <strong>QA separate</strong></td><td>If the maker inspects their own work, they miss things</td><td>Regression QA as an independent agent</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Flag</strong> it and stop when stuck</td><td>So it does not make up what it does not know</td><td>A "needs checking" flag</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Touching up the generated team</span>
    If you do not like the agents the harness made, you can just <strong>ask for changes in natural language</strong>. e.g. <code>/harness Modify the QA agent so that it must also verify the standard order cancellation scenario</code>. How solid you make the verification is what decides how much you can trust the result → <a href="#!hnp-verify">Verification · quality</a>.
</div>
